/// # The mach Plugin {#mach}
///
/// @see mim::plug::mach
///
/// [TOC]
///
/// The machine-independent surface of the native backends `x86` and `arm`.
/// A backend plugin loads it and supplies its own instructions, registers, and condition codes; this plugin holds the types, pseudo-instructions, and phases they share.
/// The phases work on the one loaded backend (`Driver::target`) and read their arguments as that backend's, e.g. `-X x86:k=<n>`.
///
/// ## Dependencies
///
plugin core;
plugin mem;
import compile;
///
/// ## Types
///
/// ### Flags
///
/// The condition flags that a comparison sets and a condition code tests.
///
axm Flags: *;
///
/// ### Spill
///
/// A spill slot holding a value of type `T`.
/// It is no register value; the emitter gives it a place in the stack frame.
///
axm Spill: * → *;
///
/// ## Registers
///
/// ### in
///
/// `in r v` is `v`, which lives in register `r` of the class of `v`'s type; `n` is the number of registers of the backend.
/// Register allocation wraps every use of a value, so all uses of `v` share one `in r v`.
/// It is no instruction: it is neither scheduled nor on the chain.
///
axm in: {n: Nat} → [r: Idx n] → {T: *} → T → T;
///
/// ## Instructions
///
/// ### slot
///
/// Reserves `n` bytes in the stack frame and yields their address.
///
axm slot: [n: Nat] → mem.M 0 → [mem.M 0, I64];
///
/// ### spill
///
/// `spill x` stores `x` into a spill slot; it is placed on the chain via `mem.sched` like any other pure instruction.
///
axm spill: {T: *} → T → Spill T;
///
/// ### reload
///
/// `reload x` loads `x` from its spill slot.
///
axm reload: {T: *} → Spill T → T;
///
/// ## Instruction Selection
///
/// ### lit
///
/// The guard `lit x` of an isel rule holds if `x` is a literal.
///
axm lit: {T: *} → T → Bool, normalize_lit;
///
/// ## Phases
///
/// ### spilling
///
/// Spills and reloads until at most `k` values of each register class are live at every point (Braun & Hack, CC 2009).
/// `k` is the number of allocatable registers of the class by default, and `-X <backend>:k=<n>` overrides it for the general-purpose one.
///
axm spilling: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### constrain
///
/// Moves the operands that an instruction needs in a fixed register, and a two-address operand that stays live, into a fresh continuation right before it.
/// The jump is the copy, so the copies' live ranges end at the instruction and the interference graph stays chordal.
/// The fixed operands are wrapped in `in`.
/// Likewise, a fixed result that stays live is passed on to a fresh continuation right after its instruction.
///
axm constrain: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### color
///
/// Assigns a register to every value by one PBQP per function and register class (Buchwald, Zwinkau, Bersch, CC 2011) and wraps each use of a value in `in`.
/// Jumps, calls, and returns are parallel copies: their arguments only prefer the registers of the parameters they bind.
/// Removes chain instructions whose results are all dead.
/// `-X <backend>:color-dot=<file>` writes the PBQP graph of each function in Graphviz DOT format, with the chosen options highlighted.
///
axm color: compile.Phase;
///
/// ### live
///
/// Prints the liveness of every scheduled function to stdout, or to `-X <backend>:live=<file>`: per block the live-in and live-out values and the maximum number of live values per register class.
/// It is not part of the default pipeline.
///
axm live: compile.Phase;
